Subject: sw_engine: translucent shapes in 8-bit (mask) buffers keep what is under their edges

cRasterTranslucentRle() and its AVX and NEON versions, for 8-bit surfaces (the buffers that mattes
and masks are drawn into), scaled the destination by the inverse of the colour's alpha instead of
the inverse of the span's alpha (colour alpha x coverage). On the anti-aliased edge of a
translucent shape drawn over an opaque one, the destination was nearly wiped: the matte got a
crack along the edge, and the content under it showed through as a thin line (Lordicon doodle
icons, whose matte pieces fade in one after another), or a whole band for larger opacity steps.
The 32-bit path already used the span's alpha.

Origin: Lordicon.
Base: thorvg/thorvg main 72a66415 (1.2.0). License: MIT, like ThorVG.
---
--- a/src/renderer/cpu_engine/tvgSwRasterCore.cpp
+++ b/src/renderer/cpu_engine/tvgSwRasterCore.cpp
@@ -198,7 +198,7 @@
             auto dst = &surface->buf8[span->y * surface->stride + x];
             if (span->coverage < 255) src = MULTIPLY(span->coverage, c.a);
             else src = c.a;
-            auto ialpha = ~c.a;
+            uint8_t ialpha = ~src;  //what the span leaves of the destination, with its coverage
             for (auto x = 0; x < len; ++x, ++dst) {
                 *dst = src + MULTIPLY(*dst, ialpha);
             }
